---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Still from '../../components/Still.astro';
import AnonPlate from '../../components/AnonPlate.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
  name: 'SED — Sistema de Evaluación Docente',
  applicationCategory: 'EducationalApplication',
  description:
    'Anonymous teacher-evaluation platform for a public university in Mexico: opaque respondent credentials, immutable submissions, Django + React, with 2,500+ automated tests.',
  operatingSystem: 'Web',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SED — Teacher Evaluation System"
  description="How I built an anonymous, tamper-evident teacher-evaluation platform for a public Mexican university: Django, React 19, opaque credentials, and 2,500+ automated tests."
  path="/work/sed/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="file-kicker">Case study · Institutional platform</p>
      <h1 class="display" style="max-width:18ch">
        Teacher evaluations, made anonymous.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        SED is the evaluation system for a public university in Mexico:
        students, teachers, and staff evaluate teaching performance every
        term — without anyone being able to trace a response back to a person.
      </p>
      <p class="chips" style="color:var(--ink-soft)">
        <span>Django + DRF</span>
        <span>React 19</span>
        <span>PostgreSQL</span>
        <span>Redis + Celery</span>
        <span>Docker / AWS</span>
      </p>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        I bring this same discipline to a small-business website: written
        scope, weekly proof, and a handover you own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem)">
    <div class="wrap media-split" style="margin-top:0">
      <Still
        src="/images/credential.webp"
        alt="Conceptual image generated for this page; not a photograph of the live system."
        width={1400}
        height={933}
        caption="A blank credential. No name. That is the point."
      />
      <div>
        <p class="file-kicker">By construction</p>
        <h2 class="h2" style="margin-top:0.4rem">How anonymity is built</h2>
        <p class="muted" style="margin:0.9rem 0 1.25rem; max-width:46ch">
          The respondent never creates an account. The file that remains
          cannot be edited.
        </p>
        <AnonPlate />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(3rem,6vw,4rem)">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        The university's previous evaluation system was a legacy stack with a
        fundamental trust problem: respondents could not be sure their answers
        were anonymous, so participation and honesty suffered. The new system
        had to guarantee anonymity <em>by construction</em>, survive real
        term-end traffic, and produce reports administrators could audit.
      </p>

      <h2>What I built</h2>
      <p>
        A full-stack platform with two completely separate faces: an admin
        console for catalogs, teaching assignments, survey instruments,
        credentials, and analytics — and a respondent flow that works with a
        single opaque credential and leaves no identifying trail.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Opaque credential system:</strong> respondents redeem a
          server-issued token for an anonymous session. No name, no account,
          no way to link a response to a person.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Immutable submissions:</strong> once an evaluation is
          submitted it cannot be edited or deleted — the dataset the
          institution reports on is provably the dataset that was collected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Survey engine:</strong> configurable instruments — matrix
          questions, Likert scales, open text, theory/practical classification
          — published per evaluation period with a strict lifecycle:
          draft → published → closed, with soft-deletes instead of hard
          erasure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Analytics &amp; reports:</strong> per-professor and
          per-unit reports for institutional decision-making.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Engineering standards</h2>
      <p>This project runs on discipline, not heroics:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1,750+ backend tests</strong> and <strong>830+ frontend tests</strong>, run in CI on every change.</li>
        <li><strong>Strict bundle budgets</strong> — every JavaScript chunk measured and capped, so slow devices in campus Wi-Fi still load fast.</li>
        <li><strong>Security review as a standing practice:</strong> auth boundaries, session handling, and data storage are audited continuously, with findings tracked and fixed, not buried.</li>
        <li><strong>Observability built in:</strong> health checks, request tracing, and metrics wired to monitoring from day one.</li>
        <li><strong>Reproducible deployment:</strong> Docker images built in CI, promoted through environments, released to AWS with an audited pipeline.</li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote>
        The requirement was never just "collect evaluations." It was:
        prove to thousands of students that their voice is safe.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Result</h2>
      <p>
        A platform the institution can run term after term: anonymous by
        design, immutable by default, and maintained through automated tests
        rather than tribal knowledge. The architecture documentation alone
        lets a new engineer onboard without a guided tour.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="padding-bottom:clamp(4rem,8vw,6rem)">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="folder folder--orchid">
        <h2 class="h2">Need a platform you can trust with sensitive data?</h2>
        <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1rem">
          Anonymity, auditability, and uptime are design decisions — not afterthoughts.
        </p>
        <a class="btn btn--solid" style="margin-top:1.75rem" href="/contact/">Talk about your project</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
